    "innominate": {
      "word": "INNOMINATE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Having no name; unnamed; as, an innominate person or place. [R.] Ray.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Anat.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "A term used in designating many parts otherwise unnamed; as, the innominate artery, a great branch of the arch of the aorta; the innominate vein, a great branch of the superior vena cava. Innominate bone (Anat.), the great bone which makes a lateral half of the pelvis in mammals; hip bone; haunch bone; huckle bone. It is composed of three bones, ilium, ischium, and pubis, consolidated into one in the adult, though separate in the fetus, as also in many adult reptiles and amphibians. specific name.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "innovate": {
      "word": "INNOVATE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To bring in as new; to introduce as a novelty; as, to innovate a word or an act. [Archaic]",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To change or alter by introducing something new; to remodel; to revolutionize. [Archaic] Burton. From his attempts upon the civil power, he proceeds to innovate God's worship. South.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To introduce novelties or changes; -- sometimes with in or on. Bacon. Every man,therefore,is not fit to innovate. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "innovation": {
      "word": "INNOVATION",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "The act of innovating; introduction of something new, in customs, rites, etc. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A change effected by innovating; a change in customs; something new, and contrary to established customs, manners, or rites. Bacon. The love of things ancient doth argue stayedness, but levity and want of experience maketh apt unto innovations. Hooker.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Bot.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A newly formed shoot, or the annually produced addition to the stems of many mosses.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
